  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 335 AM CDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nRain with embedded thunder continue to exit stage right out of \nwestern WI early this morning. For our early birds, you may have\nsome light rain to contend with early for the next couple of\nhours. However, most of if not all rainfall should have \ndiminished by sunrise. High pressure fills in behind last \nnight's activity. Increased subsidence should aid in the \nproduction of mostly sunny skies by this afternoon. \nTemperatures today will range in the lower 80s but with dew \npoints creeping back up into the upper 60s to lower 70s, heat \nindices near 90 degrees across southern MN. \n\nFor tonight, we will need to watch the progress of a warm front that \nwill reside near the MN/IA border, forecast guidance anticipates \nthat this boundary becomes stationary. A weak LLJ will stretch into \nthe southern counties of Minnesota combined with weak WAA, and ~2000 \nJ/kg of MUCAPE. This will support another night of rain and a few \nnon-severe thunderstorms across southern MN. An important caveat to \nall of this is the unknown impact of capping across this \nregion. 700mb temps overnight are forecasted to be around 12C. \nGiven the uncertainty of the capping, continued with the \nprevious shifts' thinking as well as the latest NBM advertising\n30-40% PoPs for this period.\n\nFor Thursday, the northern plains remains under upper-level ridging \nwith a deepening low over the PacNorthwest. On the eastern \nperiphery of the PacNorthwest trough, a subtle shortwave \ndevelops across eastern ND and northwestern MN. This wave is \nthen progged east over the apex of the ridge Thursday evening. \nConditions will feel hot and sticky with temperatures in the \nupper 80s and dew points in the lower 70s. Thursday night into \nearly Friday morning, rain and thunderstorms are anticipated to\ndevelop across northwestern MN and move southeast late Thursday\nafternoon/evening. The storm mode at first starts off as \nsupercellular with tornadoes, damaging winds, and large hail all\nbeing primary hazards. However, as the cells move south and \neast, they are expected to congeal into a potential MCS \ntransitioning concerns to primarily a damaging wind threat. \n\nAs we head into the Labor Day weekend, conditions will remain warm \nand sticky. Zonal flow aloft will aid in several waves to track \nacross MN/WI and allow for near daily rain/thunder chances. Highs in \nthe mid to upper 80s with dew points still in the 70s. It is not \nuntil early next week to where temperatures return to the lower 80s \nand 70s.",
